// Specialised: the naval crossing, in detail.

:: anchor=left size=20 pad=26 enter=type speed=44 line=1.4

@Signals Officer
Ships, then. This is the one that stops most people, and it stops them in the
same place every time: the army is at the coast and nothing happens.
---
@Signals Officer
An army does not cross water by itself, and there is no "load onto that ship"
order. The province does it.
---
@Signals Officer
:: point=panel.province gate=1 until=selected:own
Select a coastal province that has a §**port**§ AND §**men standing in it**§.
Embarking moves the garrison that is already there; an empty port embarks
nothing at all, which is where most of the confusion starts.

Its panel has §**Embark Army**§ -- free, one turn. Press it and end the turn.
---
@Signals Officer
:: point=view.navy gate=1 until=view:navy
The men are now a §**boat**§ off the coast. Go to the §**navy**§ view -- ships
take no orders from any of the other seven.

§{key=ship_move}§ moves it, §{key=ship_wheel}§ opens its guns,
§{key=ship_engage}§ tells it to pick a fight.
---
@Signals Officer
To land: select the boat, press §**Move**§, and click on §**land**§.

Water moves it. Land unloads it. One button does both, which is why people
sail back and forth looking for the other one.
---
@Signals Officer
Two things nobody tells you.

A loaded transport is a target, and it sinks with the men aboard. And a landing
into a defended province arrives tired -- take the empty coast next door and
walk, rather than landing on the fort.
---
@Signals Officer
§**Hallow Rock**§, in the strait, is not optional. A boat has a range, and the
far island is outside it -- so the rock is the first landing, and the second
hop goes from there.

That is ships. Anything else?
